WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.900
The most dangerous routine action on the
desk is resetting someone's sign-in method.

2
00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:09.125
After that reset, whoever registers
next controls the account.

3
00:00:09.125 --> 00:00:12.960
So a reset done on an unverified
request isn't support;

4
00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.662
it hands the account to whoever asked.

5
00:00:15.662 --> 00:00:21.760
Federal advisories describe callers posing
as employees to get exactly these resets,

6
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.724
and OMB calls recovery a potential bypass.

7
00:00:24.724 --> 00:00:27.550
The program's answer is
a verification ladder.

8
00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:31.125
Someone who's lost everything
is identity-proofed first.

9
00:00:31.125 --> 00:00:35.250
Email, caller ID and personal
questions verify no one.

10
00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.049
The decision for the
CISO: own that ladder,

11
00:00:38.049 --> 00:00:42.688
put the most friction on privileged
accounts, and alert on every reset.

12
00:00:42.688 --> 00:00:44.710
Next, why volume matters.
